Z||000000||Richard Parnell||12-11-1999||08:34 PM||||I'm going nuts.  I know this has been run over and over again.  Today I decided to bleed the clutch system since it has been getting hard to shift.  Well, I accidentally got the fluid in the master cylinder resevoir too low and sucked in air. No big deal I thought . i went and put a length of clear tubing over the slave bleed valve put it in a jar and proceeded to alternately pump the clutch, check the fluid in resevoir and top it off. then bleed the valve.  Well I've gone through almost a whole container of Dot 3. It looks like air keeps bubbling out but I suspect it may be actaully coming in the bottom of the bleed screw.  How much fluid does the whole clutch system hold.  Should I keep on pumping fluid throught there.  When I have the clutch pushed in and slightly crack the bleeder I don't see bubbles but if I open it further I see bubbles.  Auugh I'm going nuts. Help!||||reg||8
Z||000001||David||12-11-1999||08:47 PM||||Patrick, it's your time to shine again.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-11-1999||08:48 PM||||If you've gone through a container of DOT3, I would say new fluid should be completely through the system. Only places air can be getting in are:<BR>1.) through a hole in the steel line, unlikely.<BR>2.)Master cylinder allowing air in,<BR>possible.<BR>3.) Salve cylinder allowing air in,<BR>also possible.<BR>4.) The stainless steel braided rubber line that connects to the slave,<BR>again, possible, but not as likely as master or slave.<BR>Are you bleeding the system alone, or with a helper. Don't take this the wrong way, you are bleeding it correctly, right? <P>I have pumped a half quart of ATF through my system and I am still getting air bubbles.  This is with a new master and slave, but I wonder if I damaged the braided line running to the slave.<P>Another thing you can try is gravity bleeding.  Get the front end of the car high enough so the master is higher up than the slave. Open the master res. and crack open the bleeder on the slave.  Keep adding fluid to the master as it comes out the slave.<BR>Sometimes it just takes another procedure to get it right.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||12-11-1999||09:16 PM||||Fiero56, with everything tight and pumped up should I be able to pull the push rod in the slave back towards the slave cylinder?  I can grab it with my finger with the bleed valve closed and pull it towards me.||||reg||11
Z||000004||Patrick||12-11-1999||09:43 PM||||<BR>Ok, here we go again...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>First of all Richard, twenty lashes with a wet noodle for not having already switched over to <B>ATF</B>. <P>I could NOT bleed my system at all the way you are trying to do it (even if you're doing it properly) when I was still using DOT 3 because air was being sucked past the seal in my slave cylinder <B>while I was doing the bleeding</B>. If you've reviewed the old posts, you may have read a couple of times how I devised a method of forcing fluid under pressure into the system to bleed it. Even though this allowed me to drive the car for awhile, <B>the problem was not solved completely until I switched over to ATF</B>. Why are you being so stubborn?<P>To tell the honest truth, I have little sympathy for anyone who's having clutch hydraulic problems who's still using DOT 3  after all we've reported here regarding the successful conversion to ATF. What more do you need to be told to help convince you??!!!<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 12-12-1999).]||||reg||4
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||12-11-1999||10:01 PM||||Thanks guys.  The jacking up the front end and letting the fluid gravity feed routine worked.  I'm at least back where I was before.  It's still very hard to shift and I'm thinking it's time to check out the shift linkage.  Sometimes the car will shift as smooth as can be and 5 minutes later I can barely get it into different gears, but with no grinding just hard to move the shifter.  Once again the Fiero forum comes through. At least I can drie my baby.  I felt isolated there for a while. So alone.||||reg||10
Z||000006||Richard Parnell||12-11-1999||10:05 PM||||Ok.  As soon as I check out the linkage part. and I get the nerve to touch the hydraulic clutch setup again, I'll change to ATF.  Can't hurt.  I do agree , I think air is leaking in around the slave seal. I can push the slave rod in with my bare hands when everythings tightened up.||||reg||11
Z||000007||fiero56||12-11-1999||10:31 PM||||I'm not sure being able to push the slave rod in farther means much.  When you push on the clutch pedal, fluid forces the slave rod out. When you release the pedal, pressure forces the rod back in. Being able to push it in farther just means there is more travel than needed, not a sign of anything wrong with the system in general.<P>But, air getting in the system does sound like worn seals. What color was the DOT3 fluid in the master when you started?  If it was black, green or another color, seals are likely getting bad.<BR>You may get lucky and not have to replace the slave, <B> if </B>, the ATF solves the problem.  If you end up having to replace the seals, you might as well just save yourself the headache, and get a new one.  I've heard of rebuild kits working for some people, and I've heard of them not working for others.  <BR>How many miles on the system and what type of driving,(highway, city, etc)?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-11-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Richard Parnell||12-11-1999||11:07 PM||||I replaced both the slave and master several years ago when I put in a new clutch.  The first slave I bought was bad and that took me a while to figure out.  As to the color of the Fluid this time. It was black in the master reservoir.  Time to replace both slave and master?  I may as well do the ATF when I do that.  The two cylinders from NAPA are very expensive $140+ each.||||reg||6
Z||000009||fiero56||12-11-1999||11:23 PM||||I bought my master and slave from wrenchead.com. I paid $140, for the pair. I heard pricing went up a little, but still sounds better than napa. I got free shipping and two day delivery. I am pleased with them. <BR>You may not have to replace them if they are only a couple years old. Could just be old fluid that was in the line from before, working it's way up. When I pulled mine they were original. Master spring was rusted/broken in 3 pieces, obvious wear. Slave appeared to be in good condition, but I replaced it anyway. Glad to hear gravitiy bleeding helped for the time being. I have yet to try it on mine. Maybe tommorrow.<P>I just read you're from Hawaii. That may be the reason for higher pricing over there.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-11-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||mwbackus||12-12-1999||12:07 AM||||If one was to review the chemical compatibilty index offered by the various seal and o-ring manufacturers, it is easy to see why transmission fluid is not an acceptable substitute for a system designed for use with brake fluid.  The seal material used for brake fluid systems (Ethylene Propylene) is incompatible with petroleum based fluids.  The improvements you are experiencing are very likely due to the softening and swelling of the these seals caused by the transmission fluid.  I think it is safe to assume that the long term use of transmission fluid in a system that relies on Ethylene Propylene seals to work, will result in failure of these seals upon sufficient decomposition.  The reasons why some of us do not make these types of decisions based on the short term experiences of few indivduals is quite obvious.<P><BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sage||12-12-1999||12:32 AM||||MWB: You said a mouthful, and you are right!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 12-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Patrick||12-12-1999||02:52 AM||||<BR>Well Mike, suit yourself...<P>I'll let the rest of you know when my clutch hydraulic system fails. In the meantime, my clutch works better than it EVER has.<P>I'd rather have a "modified" system that works properly for a (perhaps) limited period of time, than have a (perhaps) longer lasting "proper" system with limited or no capabilities.||||reg||1
Z||000013||David||12-12-1999||11:31 AM||||MWB<P>I had thought of this problem when I made the original post saying that I was using Mobil 1 synthetic rather than DOT3.  However, even if the results are short term it was a good move.  Also, we will have to define what is "short term".  Had my daughter not totaled my Fiero, I wouldn't remove the Mobil 1 with the thought that I would be going back to a poorly operating clutch, but it would be poorer for a longer period of time than if I had used a non DOT fluid.  Time will tell.||||reg||1
Z||000014||RZURCTN||12-12-1999||10:25 PM||||Good point on the seal compatability.  Something else you guys with ATF may try is replacing old seals with a kind more suited to your ATF.  Another thing I thought of (and this is from experience) is that ATF or Dexron, when mixed with a silicon based fluid, creates a gel that can cause a failure in your hydraulic system.  In the Marine Corps we have a forklift that uses Dexron for the brake fluid.  Somebody thought that since it was a brake system they would put "brake fluid" into it.  Long story short,  NO BRAKES!!!  Had to rebuild both axles entirely! And replace all system components.  Just my two cents worth!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by RZURCTN (edited 12-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||DJRice||12-13-1999||02:50 PM||||OK, so Rzurctn is in charge of making everyone new master cylinder resevoir caps that say <B>Clutch Fill - ATF Dextron II/III ONLY</B><p>[This message has been edited by DJRice (edited 12-13-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Richard Parnell||12-13-1999||04:07 PM||||Thanks everyone for your help this weekend.  I got the car at least running but it's still a bear to get into first, reverse and second.  The other gears sometimes smooth sometimes not.  I ordered new master and slave from wrenchead and bought some ATF.  I'm going to try and bleed the setup I have right now with the DOT3 ONE LAST TIME, before I get all the new parts in.  I feel like I'm about to break the shifter or cable it's so hard to shift.  When I'm stopped at a light in first and let off the brakes the cars creeps forward. I am really hesitant about putting in the V8 until I figure this mess out once and for all.  It's either that or go to the Caddy install w/auto.  But I just don't like the thought of driving my Fiero without shifting it.||||reg||9
Z||000017||Patrick||12-13-1999||04:40 PM||||<BR>Richard, I made my earlier suggestion to not only try and help you get your car working properly, but to also try and save you some money. If you plan on switching over to <B>ATF</B> anyway, why don't you do it BEFORE you change your master and slave cylinders? You may find that you don't need the new units. If nothing else, you'll at least get the hydraulic line nicely flushed out before you put in the new components (if you need them).<P>It could conceivably cost you a grand total of <B>TWO DOLLARS</B> and a half hour of your time to eliminate these problems.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Eric||12-13-1999||04:44 PM||||I know what you mean Richard.  Everything I do to my car never turns out as well as it should because the stupid clutch pedal is so freaky.  I don't think the problem will ever be resolved until we accept the Fiero clutch system just plain sucks.  I've always driven cars with manual transmissions and I can't think of driving anything else.  I realize there are people out there who love their autos.  Maybe we can learn to love an auto too.  I know it's a hard thing to do Richard, but I think you should look into a Northstar conversion.  And when you're feeling down about not being able to throw your own gears you can always go out and smoke your tires.  Good luck, man.||||reg||9
Z||000019||David||12-13-1999||05:17 PM||||Richard<BR>I'll second Patrick's last post.  Of the three master cylinders that I had on my car, the last one was the worst, and it was only 6 weeks old.  A GM cyl, not an aftermatket.  With nothing to loose I put Molil 1 in the system and this terrible acting M/C instantly became perfect and stayed that way for as long as I had the car, about 5 months after I replaced the DOT3.  Try the AFT before you spend any money on new parts.||||reg||1
